.TH TOPS 1 "September 25, 2026" "LibreDarwin"
.SH NAME
\fBtops\fR \- in-place textual substitution tool for source files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tops
[\fB\-help\fR] [\fB\-dont\fR] [\fB\-semiverbose\fR] [\fB\-verbose\fR]
[\fB\-nocontext\fR] [\fB\-nofileinfo\fR]
[\fB\-scriptfile\fR \fIscript\fR] [\fB\-classfile\fR \fIclassfile\fR]
[\fB\-find\fR \fIpattern\fR]
[\fB\-replace\fR \fIpattern\fR \fIwith\fR \fIreplacement\fR]
[\fB\-replacemethod\fR \fIselector\fR \fIwith\fR \fInew-selector\fR]
[\fIfilenames\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tops
rewrites files in place by applying a sequence of textual rules, and reports
what it found.  It is built for source trees: patterns are matched at the
token level, comments and string literals are treated as opaque, and named
tokens can be used to constrain or nest rules.
.PP
Rules may be given on the command line, as operands of
.BR find ,
.B replace
and
.BR replacemethod ,
or in a script file named with
.BR \-scriptfile .
Both are turned into the same text and parsed by the same parser, so the two
forms accept the same rules.  A rule keyword may be written with or without a
leading dash, and an operand given on the command line may be passed bare,
because each operand is quoted before it is parsed.  In a script file the
operands must themselves be quoted.
.PP
Each
.I filename
is read, every rule is applied in the order given, and the result is written
back only if some rule changed it.  With no
.I filename
the text is read from standard input and the transformed text is echoed to
standard output instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-help
Print the usage summary and exit.
.TP
.B \-dont
Do not write any file.  The change that would have been made is still
reported, even when the run is quiet, so a dry run shows its work.
.TP
.B \-semiverbose
Print the per-file banner and the occurrence count for each file.
.TP
.B \-verbose
Also print every match of a
.B replace
or
.B replacemethod
rule, as
.IR file : line : "'" old "' -> '" new "'" .
.TP
.B \-nocontext
Drop the matching line from the report of a
.B find
rule and report only the text that matched, as
.IR file : line : matched-text .
Reports of
.B replace
and
.B replacemethod
keep their line context.
.TP
.B \-nofileinfo
Omit the file name and line number from the reports of a
.B find
rule, leaving only the text itself.  The reports of
.B replace
and
.B replacemethod
keep them, and the per-file banner and the occurrence count are unaffected.
.TP
.BI \-scriptfile " script"
Read the rules from
.IR script .
.SH "SCRIPT FILE FORMAT"
A script file is a list of rules, one per line.  A rule keyword may carry a
leading dash.  A comma does not separate rules: it ends the parser, which
reports the rest of the line as an error.  The grammar is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
find <search_pattern> [where (<symbol>...) isOneOf {(<match>...)...}]...

replace <search_pattern> with <replacement_pattern> | same
    [where (<symbol>...) isOneOf {(<match>...)...}]...
    [within (<symbol>) {rules...}]...
    [error <message>] [warning <message>]

replacemethod <selector> with <new_selector>
    { [replace <symbol> with <symbol_replacement>]... }
    [where (<symbol>...) isOneOf {(<match>...)...}]...
    [within (<symbol>) {rules...}]...
    [error <message>] [warning <message>]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B with
keyword between the two patterns of a
.B replace
rule is optional, so
.B replace "a" "b"
and
.B replace "a" with "b"
are the same rule.  A
.B replacemethod
selector must be quoted, as must every operand of a
.B where
clause.
.SH RULES
.TP
.BI find " pattern"
Report every match of
.I pattern
and leave the file alone.
.TP
.BI replace " pattern" " with " replacement
Replace every match of
.I pattern
with
.IR replacement .
Writing
.B same
instead of a replacement leaves the matched text unchanged and reports
nothing: the rule is a no-op that neither counts nor rewrites.
.TP
.BI replacemethod " selector" " with " new-selector
Rename the colon-terminated parts of an Objective-C method message that
appears in
.RB \(oq [obj\ sel:1] \(cq
or
.RB \(oq [obj\ sel:2] \(cq
style context lines.  A pattern part is matched as a prefix and the trailing
colon is part of the pattern, so a part with no colon never matches, and the
message may carry more parts than the pattern but never fewer.  A
.I new-selector
is truncated at its last colon.  Inside the braces, each
.BI replace " symbol" " with " symbol-replacement
renames one part, a missing argument becomes
.BR <_tops_argN> ,
and a part name shorter than two characters is dropped from the output while
keeping its slot.
.TP
.BI error " message"
.TQ
.BI warning " message"
Insert a comment line at the start of every source line the rule matches,
.B #error
for
.B error
and
.B #warning
for
.BR warning ,
one such line per match, so a line matched n times gains n comment lines.
When a rule carries both, only the
.B #error
line is inserted.  Each inserted line is itself counted, so the reported
number of occurrences is twice the number of matches.  A rule that matches
nothing inserts nothing, and the
.I message
itself is never printed.  These clauses are not accepted on a
.B find
rule, which reports a parse error instead.
.SH PATTERNS
Patterns are matched as tokens, not as raw text.  A word is a run of letters,
digits, underscores and dollar signs, and any other character delimits words,
so the pattern
.B foo
matches inside
.BR foo; ,
.B foo = bar
and
.BR [foo:1] ,
but not inside
.BR foobar .
Whitespace in a pattern matches one or more whitespace characters, so
.B "int a"
matches
.BR "int  a" ,
while whitespace at the start or end of a pattern is ignored.
.PP
Text inside a comment or a quoted literal is never matched, replaced or
reported.  The recognized regions are
.BR // " to end of line,"
.BR /* " to */,"
and single- or double-quoted literals with backslash escapes.  An
unterminated
.B /*
runs to the end of the input.
.PP
A name in angle brackets,
.BR <foo> ,
matches any run of text and is captured.  A captured name can be named in a
.B where
or
.B within
clause, and the full spelling, including the angle brackets and any type
label, is the name to use:
.PP
.RS
.nf
replace "foo=<e x>" with "X" where ("<e x>") isOneOf {("1")}
.fi
.RE
.SH "WHERE AND WITHIN"
.BI where " (" symbols ")" " isOneOf {" tuples "}"
restricts a match to the values its pattern captured.  Each symbol is the
full text of a named token, so
.B ("<e x>")
refers to a token written
.B <e x>
in the pattern.  Each tuple lists one value per symbol, and a clause passes
when any one of its tuples equals the captured values exactly.  Several
.B where
clauses may be given and all of them must pass.  A clause that names a token
the pattern did not capture is ignored, so a pattern without
.B <e x>
is unaffected by a clause about it.
.PP
.BI within " (" symbol ") {" rules "}"
runs a nested set of rules inside the text captured by a named token, which
is how a method argument or a bracketed value is rewritten on its own.
.SH REPORTING
The reports of a
.B find
rule are printed in every mode, including the default quiet one, because
.B find
never changes a file.  The reports of a
.B replace
or
.B replacemethod
rule are printed only under
.B \-verbose
or
.BR \-dont ,
as
.IR file : line : "'" old "' -> '" new "'" ;
.B \-nocontext
and
.B \-nofileinfo
reshape those two lines as described above.
.PP
The per-file banner
.BI "Processing " file
and the total
.IB file ": " N " occurrences found"
need
.B \-semiverbose
or
.BR \-verbose ;
.B \-dont
alone does not print them, because it changes what is done rather than how
much is said.  A file that was rewritten is announced as
.IB file " written"
under the same two options, and never with
.BR \-dont ,
which writes nothing.
.PP
Standard input is echoed line by line, with the reports interleaved, and
.B \-verbose
adds a final count of
.IR N " occurrences" .
.PP
When standard output is a terminal, a two-row progress bar is drawn whose
lower row fills as the input is consumed, and each dot is flushed
immediately.  Reports and banners go to standard output; only failures to
read or write a file are reported on standard error.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B tops
exits 0 in every case, including a rule that matches nothing, a script it
cannot parse and a file that does not exist.  The last two print a diagnostic
and the usage summary on standard output, and a missing class hierarchy is
reported as
.B ***Bad class hierarchy
on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Report every occurrence of a symbol in a file, without changing it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tops -find dealloc *.m
.fi
.RE
.PP
Rename a selector across a source tree, writing the changes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tops -verbose -replacemethod "oldName:" with "newName:" *.m
.fi
.RE
.PP
Load rules from a script file, and see the changes without writing them:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tops -dont -verbose -scriptfile rename.script *.m
.fi
.RE
.PP
A script file holding the same rename:
.PP
.RS
.nf
replacemethod "oldName:" with "newName:"
replace "<e x>" with "X" within ("<e x>") { replace "tmp" with "cache" }
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR open (1),
.BR pbcopy (1),
.BR pbpaste (1)
